  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/afa5a80d73d9d8511cc35804277ad9dc3f1b62b2e1dbe3911d6ea9d665c3baa8.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(5,3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$ \n$\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061($ {{field-1}} \n$).$",
  "worked_solution": "The angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{3}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, the polar equation is  \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,).\n$$",
